/* NAT IELTS Writing Task 2 — curriculum extension, original model essays. */
window.NAT_TASK2_ESSAYS={"games":{"topic":"Children spending more time on video games than sports","prompt":"Children nowadays spend considerably more time playing video games than participating in sports. Why is this happening? Is it a positive or negative development?","origin":"PDF 01, tr. 3–4: bối cảnh và hai câu hỏi; bài mẫu viết mới","paras":["Children now devote a growing share of their leisure time to digital games rather than physical activities. This shift is largely driven by the convenience of online entertainment and a shortage of accessible recreational spaces, and I consider its overall impact negative despite certain benefits.","One reason for this preference is that gaming offers instant entertainment with remarkably few practical barriers. A child can play with friends at home regardless of the weather, whereas joining a sports team often requires transport, equipment and an organised timetable. Moreover, in densely populated cities, safe parks and affordable sports facilities may be difficult to reach. When outdoor opportunities are limited, a screen becomes the easiest option, particularly for parents who are concerned about traffic or neighbourhood safety. These circumstances make gaming more attractive even to children who might otherwise enjoy sport.","Although some games develop strategic thinking and cooperation, the displacement of regular exercise is troubling. Physical activity contributes to cardiovascular fitness, sleep quality and social confidence, none of which can be fully replaced by online interaction. Children who spend most evenings sitting indoors may also miss chances to handle disagreements and collaborate face to face. For example, participation in a local football club requires young players to communicate, respond to setbacks and accept shared responsibility. The issue is therefore not that gaming has no value, but that excessive gaming can crowd out experiences that support balanced development.","In conclusion, digital games are appealing because they are convenient and accessible, particularly where outdoor facilities are inadequate. However, their growing dominance over physical activity is, on balance, an unhealthy development, and families and communities should make active alternatives easier to pursue."]},"family":{"topic":"The disappearance of shared family meals","prompt":"In many societies, families are spending less time eating meals together. What are the main reasons for this trend, and what effects can it have on family relationships?","origin":"PDF 02, tr. 1 & 4: hiện tượng và đoạn mẫu; câu hỏi được biên soạn bổ sung","paras":["Shared family meals have become less common in many households, a change that reflects broader shifts in working patterns and daily routines. This trend stems primarily from conflicting schedules and the increasing attraction of individualised entertainment, and it can gradually weaken communication and emotional closeness within families.","The most immediate cause is that family members no longer follow the same timetable. Parents may work irregular hours or travel long distances to their jobs, while children attend extra classes and after-school activities. Even when everybody is at home, meals are sometimes replaced by convenience food eaten at different times. Digital devices compound the problem: instead of gathering around the table, individuals may prefer to watch programmes or interact with friends online. Consequently, eating together comes to be regarded as an optional activity rather than a reliable part of the day.","The decline in shared meals can affect relationships in subtle but cumulative ways. Mealtimes provide an informal opportunity for parents and children to exchange news, notice changes in mood and resolve minor misunderstandings before they become serious. Without this regular contact, family members may know less about one another's concerns, making conversations more superficial. For younger children, the absence of a predictable family routine can also reduce the sense of support and belonging that develops through ordinary, repeated interactions. These consequences are not inevitable, but they become more likely when families lack other occasions for meaningful communication.","In conclusion, demanding schedules and screen-based leisure have contributed to the decline of family meals. Although households can maintain strong relationships in other ways, losing this shared routine may reduce everyday communication and, over time, create greater emotional distance."]},"century":{"topic":"Quality of life in the twenty-first century","prompt":"People living in the 21st century have a better quality of life than those who lived in previous centuries. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","origin":"PDF 03, tr. 1–3: đề gốc; bài mẫu mới","paras":["It is often argued that people today enjoy a higher standard of living than those in earlier centuries. I largely agree with this view because advances in public health and technology have substantially improved everyday life, although these benefits remain unevenly distributed.","The clearest improvement has occurred in healthcare and public sanitation. Vaccination programmes, safer drinking water and effective treatments have reduced the risks associated with diseases that once claimed large numbers of lives. Modern medicine has also made many conditions manageable rather than immediately life-threatening. Importantly, these gains are not limited to medical procedures: better understanding of nutrition and preventive care enables people to make more informed decisions about their wellbeing. Compared with previous generations, many individuals therefore have a greater chance of living a longer and healthier life.","Technological progress has likewise expanded what people can do with their time and resources. Digital communication allows families separated by distance to remain in contact, while online learning and financial services reduce the need to travel for routine tasks. In addition, labour-saving devices have made essential domestic work less physically demanding. These changes create opportunities for education, employment and leisure that were unavailable to most people in earlier centuries. Nevertheless, greater convenience should not be mistaken for universal prosperity: poverty, environmental pressures and unequal access to technology continue to diminish many people's quality of life.","In conclusion, I believe the twenty-first century offers better living conditions overall, mainly because of improvements in healthcare and the opportunities made possible by technology. However, the full value of this progress depends on whether its benefits are shared more fairly."]},"money":{"topic":"Financial education at school","prompt":"Some people believe that students should be taught how to manage money at school. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","origin":"PDF 03, tr. 5: đề luyện tập 2; bài mẫu mới","paras":["Financial literacy is increasingly regarded as a skill that schools should teach alongside traditional academic subjects. I strongly agree, as early instruction can help young people avoid costly mistakes and make financial decisions more independently in adult life.","First, learning to budget at school would give pupils a practical framework for managing limited resources. Young adults are frequently required to choose between immediate spending and longer-term priorities, yet many leave school without understanding interest, debt or the value of regular savings. Simple classroom activities could address this gap by asking students to allocate a fictional monthly income among housing, food, transport and savings. Such exercises would demonstrate that every purchase involves a trade-off and that small financial decisions can accumulate into significant consequences. This understanding is especially valuable before students begin using credit or earning a salary.","Financial education would also protect young people from misleading commercial claims. Online advertisements and financial influencers increasingly promote products that appear attractive but involve hidden costs or considerable risk. Students who can compare loan terms, recognise unrealistic returns and distinguish needs from wants are better positioned to evaluate such offers critically. Schools need not introduce a separate examination-heavy subject to achieve this; financial concepts could be incorporated into mathematics, citizenship or project-based learning. In this way, pupils could practise sound judgement without sacrificing essential academic content.","In conclusion, I support teaching money management in schools because it builds responsible spending habits and improves students' ability to assess financial risks. A practical, age-appropriate programme would equip them for decisions they will inevitably face beyond the classroom."]},"jobs":{"topic":"Working for one employer versus changing jobs","prompt":"Some people believe that employees should remain with the same organisation throughout their working lives, while others think it is better to work for different organisations. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","origin":"PDF 01, tr. 3: đề thảo luận; bài mẫu mới","paras":["Whether people should build their careers within one organisation or move between employers is a question with reasonable arguments on both sides. While long-term employment can provide stability and specialised expertise, I believe that changing organisations at appropriate stages often creates better opportunities for professional growth.","Those who favour remaining with one employer emphasise the advantages of continuity. Employees who spend many years in the same organisation usually gain a detailed understanding of its systems, clients and workplace culture. This knowledge can make them highly effective and may qualify them for internal promotion. Long-term service can also support strong professional relationships, which are particularly valuable in sectors where trust and institutional knowledge matter. For workers with family commitments, the predictability of a stable role may be more attractive than the uncertainty of a new position.","On the other hand, moving between organisations can expose workers to different approaches and prevent their skills from becoming too narrow. A software developer, for instance, might learn more by contributing to projects in several industries than by repeatedly maintaining one system. Changing jobs can also provide opportunities for higher pay, greater responsibility or a healthier working environment when these are unavailable internally. Nevertheless, frequent moves without a clear purpose may weaken a professional's record of achievement. The strongest approach is therefore to change employers when the move offers meaningful development rather than simply pursuing novelty.","In conclusion, loyalty to one organisation offers security and valuable experience, but I favour carefully chosen career moves because they can broaden expertise and open opportunities that long-term employment in one position may not provide."]},"behaviour":{"topic":"Deteriorating student behaviour","prompt":"In some schools, students are showing increasingly disruptive behaviour. What are the main causes of this problem, and what measures could be taken to address it?","origin":"PDF 04, tr. 3–4: lax parental discipline / classroom management; đề đầy đủ được biên soạn bổ sung","paras":["Disruptive behaviour has become a serious concern in some schools, affecting both learning and the wider classroom environment. In many cases, inconsistent expectations at home and inadequate support for classroom management contribute to the problem; a coordinated response from families and schools is therefore essential.","One underlying cause is that children may receive conflicting messages about acceptable conduct. If rules are rarely explained or applied consistently at home, pupils can find it difficult to respect boundaries at school. Another factor is the pressure placed on teachers who manage large classes without sufficient training or pastoral support. When minor incidents are handled inconsistently, disruptive behaviour may spread because pupils receive little guidance about its consequences. These problems are particularly pronounced when schools and parents communicate only after a serious incident has occurred.","Effective solutions need to address both causes. Parents can establish a small number of clear, age-appropriate expectations and reinforce them through consistent responses rather than harsh punishment. At the same time, schools should provide teachers with practical training in de-escalation, restorative conversations and positive classroom routines. Regular communication between staff and families would help ensure that a pupil receives compatible guidance in both settings. For example, a short weekly progress report and agreed behaviour targets could allow adults to recognise improvements while responding promptly to recurring difficulties. Additional counselling support may be necessary where misconduct reflects emotional distress or unmet learning needs.","In conclusion, disruptive behaviour often stems from inconsistent boundaries and limited classroom-management support. Clearer expectations at home, better teacher preparation and sustained cooperation between families and schools offer a more constructive response than punishment alone."]},"cctv":{"topic":"Advantages and disadvantages of video surveillance","prompt":"In many cities, more video cameras are being installed in public places to reduce crime, but some people believe this restricts individual freedom. Do the advantages of video surveillance outweigh its disadvantages?","origin":"PDF 05, tr. 4: chủ đề và câu hỏi gốc; bài mẫu mới, không sao chép bài Simon","paras":["The growing use of surveillance cameras in public spaces has raised concerns about the balance between security and personal freedom. Although widespread monitoring creates genuine privacy risks, I believe its advantages can outweigh the disadvantages when cameras are subject to clear legal safeguards and independent oversight.","The strongest objection is that constant surveillance may erode people's sense of privacy. Individuals can feel uncomfortable knowing that ordinary activities are recorded and potentially analysed without their knowledge. More seriously, footage collected for crime prevention could be misused for unrelated purposes or retained for longer than necessary. There is also a risk that authorities will become overly dependent on cameras while neglecting community policing, which is essential for building public trust. These concerns make unrestricted surveillance difficult to justify.","Nevertheless, carefully regulated cameras can provide substantial public benefits. Their visible presence may deter opportunistic offences in busy transport hubs and shopping areas, while recordings can help investigators reconstruct incidents more accurately. Footage may also protect innocent people by providing evidence that challenges an unreliable accusation. Crucially, these benefits do not require unlimited monitoring: clear signs, restricted access to recordings, short retention periods and independent review can reduce the likelihood of abuse. When such protections are enforced, surveillance becomes a targeted safety measure rather than an open-ended intrusion into private life.","In conclusion, video surveillance can threaten individual freedom if it operates without accountability. However, its ability to deter certain crimes and support fair investigations makes it beneficial overall, provided that privacy protections are built into its use."]}};
